---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const BASE = import.meta.env.BASE_URL?.replace(/\/$/, "") || "";
function link(href: string) {
  return href === "/" ? BASE : BASE + href;
}
---

<Layout title="Ceph Benchmarks Deep Dive — What 99 OSDs Across 4 Storage Classes Deliver">
  <article class="blog-post">
    <header>
      <p class="blog-meta">September 20, 2026 · David Gutman</p>
      <h1>Ceph Benchmarks Deep Dive</h1>
      <p class="blog-dek">
        Real-world rados bench results for a heterogeneous Ceph cluster running
        HDD, SSD, NVMe, and Optane-backed OSDs — with 3× replication, MTU 9000,
        and the benchmark bugs that nearly sent us down the wrong path.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>The Cluster</h2>

    <p>
      Our main Ceph cluster (<strong>pmoxprime</strong>) runs Ceph Squid 19.2.4
      across 14 Proxmox nodes. It's the opposite of a uniform deployment — 99
      OSDs spread across four device classes with a CRUSH hierarchy designed to
      keep each tier isolated:
    </p>

    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>OSDs</th><th>Device</th><th>Pools</th><th>Target Workload</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>HDD</strong></td><td>53</td><td>6–12 TB SATA (Seagate)</td><td>ceph_bulk, cephfs_images_bulk, profile_hdd</td><td>Capacity — bulk storage, WSI archive</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>SSD</strong></td><td>28</td><td>3.49 TB SATA (Samsung/Micron)</td><td>ceph_ssd</td><td>Balanced — general-purpose VM storage</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>NVMe</strong></td><td>10</td><td>1.6 TB Intel P3600</td><td>ceph_nvme, profile_nvme</td><td>Performance — low latency, high IOPS</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Optane-Backed</strong></td><td>8</td><td>3.49 TB SATA SSD + 64 GB NVMe DB</td><td>ceph_optane</td><td>Low-latency — metadata-heavy workloads</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      The key architectural decision: <strong>every HDD has its WAL on NVMe</strong>
      via BlueFS dedicated DB. The SSDoptane class pairs a SATA SSD data device
      with a dedicated 64 GB NVMe partition for BlueFS DB, with WAL co-located.
      This offload architecture is what makes the HDD tier competitive for small
      writes.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>Benchmark Methodology</h2>

    <p>
      All numbers here come from <code>rados bench</code> — Ceph's built-in
      benchmarking tool. Parameters:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>30-second test duration per object size</li>
      <li>16 concurrent threads (limited by rados bench defaults)</li>
      <li>3× replication across all pools (size=3, min_size=2)</li>
      <li>MTU 9000 on the Ceph public and cluster networks</li>
      <li>Tests run from a monitor node (triton) against dedicated profile pools</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <strong>⚠️ Important caveats:</strong> rados bench uses a single OSD
      thread per rados instance and limited queue depth. These numbers
      <em>underreport</em> what the drives can do compared to a properly
      multi-threaded workload. They're useful for comparing tiers, not for
      absolute drive benchmarking. True peak performance would be 2-4× higher
      with optimal queue depth and parallel rados instances.
    </div>

    <hr />

    <h2>Large Object Performance (4 MB)</h2>

    <p>
      For large sequential objects, the network is often the bottleneck, not
      the drives. Here's how each tier compares:
    </p>

    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Class</th><th>Write BW</th><th>Write IOPS</th><th>Read BW</th><th>Read IOPS</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>NVMe</strong></td>
        <td class="num">1,175 MB/s</td>
        <td class="num">293</td>
        <td class="num accent">1,175 MB/s</td>
        <td class="num">294</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>SSD</strong></td>
        <td class="num">431 MB/s</td>
        <td class="num">107</td>
        <td class="num accent">1,170 MB/s</td>
        <td class="num">292</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>HDD</strong></td>
        <td class="num">346 MB/s</td>
        <td class="num">86</td>
        <td class="num">852 MB/s</td>
        <td class="num">213</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>📊 Key observation:</strong> SSD and NVMe reads are virtually
      identical at ~1,170 MB/s — both saturate the 25 GbE network. HDD reads
      at 852 MB/s, which is excellent for spinning rust and reflects the DB/WAL
      offload doing its job. Write throughput tells a different story: the
      NVMe tier writes at 2.7× the SSD tier, and the HDD tier holds its own at
      346 MB/s — about 80% of the SSD write rate.
    </div>

    <hr />

    <h2>Small Object IOPS (4 KB) — Where Tiers Actually Separate</h2>

    <p>
      This is the test that reveals real performance differences. Small-object
      IOPS are dominated by seek time, controller overhead, and the effectiveness
      of the BlueFS DB/WAL offload:
    </p>

    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Class</th><th>Write BW</th><th>Write IOPS</th><th>Read BW</th><th>Read IOPS</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>NVMe</strong></td>
          <td class="num">32.5 MB/s</td>
          <td class="num highlight">8,320</td>
          <td class="num">125 MB/s</td>
          <td class="num highlight">32,024</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>SSD</strong></td>
          <td class="num">7.8 MB/s</td>
          <td class="num">1,999</td>
          <td class="num accent">147 MB/s</td>
          <td class="num highlight">37,629</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>HDD</strong></td>
          <td class="num">9.0 MB/s</td>
          <td class="num highlight-alt">2,299</td>
          <td class="num">94 MB/s</td>
          <td class="num">23,988</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>🔍 The HDD surprise:</strong> HDDs achieve <strong>2,299 write
      IOPS</strong> — <em>higher</em> than SATA SSDs at 1,999 IOPS. This is
      entirely due to the NVMe WAL offload. The write penalty of spinning
      rust is almost eliminated because small writes land on the NVMe WAL
      and are committed asynchronously. For read IOPS, SSDs lead at 37,629
      vs HDD's 23,988 — a narrower gap than you'd expect, again thanks to
      the NVMe DB accelerating metadata lookups.
    </div>

    <h3>Understanding the NVMe Write IOPS Number</h3>

    <p>
      8,320 write IOPS might seem low for NVMe. Here's the context:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>3× replication:</strong> every write to Ceph is stored on three OSDs. The raw drive write IOPS is ~25,000 per drive — reasonable for Intel P3600 (PCIe 3.0) drives from 2015.</li>
      <li><strong>rados bench at 16 threads</strong> is the bottleneck here. These NVMe drives can handle queue depths of 64–128+. With a properly tuned workload, expect 2-3× these numbers.</li>
      <li><strong>32 PGs for 8 OSDs</strong> limits parallelism. Increasing to 128–256 PGs would distribute load better.</li>
      <li><strong>The Intel P3600</strong> is a PCIe 3.0 enterprise NVMe drive — good for its era (~2,600 MB/s sequential, ~460K random read IOPS at QD128), but not a modern Gen 4 drive.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h2>Read Performance: Sequential vs Random</h2>

    <p>
      An interesting detail: Ceph's <code>rados bench</code> with sequential and
      random read flags showed <strong>identical performance</strong> for 4 MB
      objects across all tiers. This makes sense — at 4 MB objects with 3×
      replication, Ceph's CRUSH distribution spreads the replicas across OSDs
      such that sequential access patterns don't give the read head a meaningful
      advantage. The bottleneck is network throughput, not disk seek:
    </p>

    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Class</th><th>Seq Read (4 MB)</th><th>Rand Read (4 MB)</th><th>Difference</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>NVMe</td><td class="num">1,175 MB/s</td><td class="num">1,175 MB/s</td><td>0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SSD</td><td class="num">1,170 MB/s</td><td class="num">1,170 MB/s</td><td>0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HDD</td><td class="num">852 MB/s</td><td class="num">852 MB/s</td><td>0%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      For 4 KB objects, random reads were slightly slower than sequential — the
      HDD tier dropped from ~24K to ~19K IOPS — but the gap was smaller than
      we expected, again reflecting the DB/WAL offload absorbing the rotational
      penalty.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>The Benchmark Bug That Almost Derailed Us</h2>

    <p>
      An important meta-lesson: our automated benchmark script was <strong>wrong</strong>
      for weeks. The script parsed <code>rados bench</code> output looking for
      <code>"IOPS:"</code>, but the actual output uses <code>"Average IOPS:"</code>.
      Every IOPS value the script reported was silently zero or incorrect.
    </p>

    <p>
      We discovered this when manual <code>rados bench</code> runs gave completely
      different — and much better — numbers. The lesson: <strong>always verify
      automated benchmark results with manual runs</strong>. A parsing bug can
      hide for months if nobody questions the output.
    </p>

    <p>
      The corrected numbers told a much better story. The HDD tier went from
      looking mediocre to genuinely impressive once the DB/WAL offload was
      properly reflected in the IOPS figures.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>MTU 9000: The 10× Improvement</h2>

    <p>
      Early in the cluster's life, we ran with standard MTU 1500 on the Ceph
      network. After switching to MTU 9000 (jumbo frames), we saw dramatic
      improvements — roughly <strong>10× for small-block workloads</strong> and
      significant gains for large-block transfers.
    </p>

    <p>
      The mechanism: with MTU 9000, each Ethernet frame carries 6× more payload.
      For a 4 MB write, that's ~290 frames instead of ~2,800 at MTU 1500. Less
      CPU time spent on interrupt handling, fewer context switches, and the
      25 GbE links can actually reach line rate instead of being frame-bound.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every Ceph node in our cluster now has MTU 9000 on dedicated storage
      VLANs (172.16.25.0/24 for Ceph backup, 192.168.24.0/24 for Ceph public
      where configured). The one lesson: <strong>verify end-to-end</strong> —
      a single misconfigured switch port will cause packet loss and
      performance degradation that's hard to diagnose.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>Optane-Backed Tier: Work in Progress</h2>

    <p>
      The SSDoptane class (8 OSDs) pairs SATA SSDs with dedicated 64 GB NVMe
      partitions for BlueFS DB. We haven't published benchmark numbers for this
      tier yet because early tests revealed <strong>configuration issues</strong>
      with the pool that skewed results. The pool is being rebuilt for proper
      characterization.
    </p>

    <p>
      What we expect: the 64 GB NVMe DB device should accelerate metadata
      operations and small writes dramatically compared to the plain SSD tier,
      while the SATA SSD provides 3.49 TB of capacity per OSD. This tier should
      slot between SSD and NVMe in the latency hierarchy — close to NVMe for
      metadata-heavy workloads, close to SSD for bulk throughput.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>Cluster-Wide Summary</h2>

    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Ceph Version</td><td>Squid 19.2.4</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Total OSDs</td><td>99 (53 HDD + 28 SSD + 10 NVMe + 8 Optane-backed)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Raw Capacity</td><td>~451 TiB total, ~345 TiB available</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Placement Groups</td><td>1,601 (all active+clean)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pools</td><td>12 (5 replicated + 1 erasure-coded + profile/test pools)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CRUSH Rules</td><td>9 (4 device-class + 3 replicated + 2 EC)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Network</td><td>25 GbE with MTU 9000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Best 4 MB Write</td><td>1,175 MB/s (NVMe)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Best 4 KB Write IOPS</td><td>8,320 (NVMe)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Best 4 KB Read IOPS</td><td>37,629 (SSD)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <hr />

    <h2>What We'd Do Differently</h2>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Higher PG counts.</strong> 32 PGs for 8 NVMe OSDs leaves
        performance on the table. 128–256 PGs would improve parallelism,
        especially for small-object workloads.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Benchmark with proper queue depth.</strong> rados bench at
        16 threads underreports NVMe capability. A multi-instance fio workload
        via <code>rbd map</code> or librbd would give more realistic numbers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Standardize MTU 9000 everywhere.</strong> Not all nodes have
        the Ceph public VLAN configured — some fall back to the management
        network at MTU 1500, creating a mixed-MTU situation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fix the benchmark script early.</strong> Weeks of development
        decisions were based on bad data because a grep string was wrong.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Characterize the Optane tier before putting it in production.</strong>
        We rushed the deployment and discovered config issues during benchmarking
        that should have been caught earlier.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <hr />

    <h2>How to Run This Yourself</h2>

    <pre><code># Quick rados bench on any Ceph pool
rados -p &lt;pool&gt; bench 30 write -b 4194304 --no-cleanup
rados -p &lt;pool&gt; bench 30 seq -b 4194304
rados -p &lt;pool&gt; bench 30 rand -b 4194304

# 4K small-object test
rados -p &lt;pool&gt; bench 30 write -b 4096 --no-cleanup
rados -p &lt;pool&gt; bench 30 rand -b 4096

# Clean up test objects
rados -p &lt;pool&gt; cleanup</code></pre>

    <p>
      For a comprehensive campaign across multiple tiers, our
      <a href="https://github.com/dagutman/Infra-brain">INFRA-BRAIN</a>
      repository has a benchmark toolkit with automated campaign scripts.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>Related Reading</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={link("/frankencluster/")}>The Pathology Frankencluster</a>
        — full hardware inventory, network topology, and all storage tiers
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={link("/blog/icarus-optimization/")}>How 3 ZFS Settings Cost Us 11× Performance</a>
        — our TrueNAS tuning story with lessons for any ZFS deployment
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Layout>

